title: Interdisciplinary Studies SBA, Part 1 - High School Dropout Analysis
authors: EDUC 3100 Assessment Module
~~~~

Orientation [id=orientation]
-----------

Omer: Hi. This is the module on interdisciplinary reasoning for EDUC 3100 that I spoke about during one of our classes.

Omer: It should take you about an hour to complete, and you can break it up over several days if you'd like. However, please make sure you finish it by end of Sunday, April 13, 2025.

Omer: In this module, you'll complete a simulated project in which you'll apply what you've learned in class about interdisciplinary analysis and the broad model of interdisciplinary research. The module contains everything you need to complete the project.

Omer: There are instructions on each screen to help you navigate and give your answers. There are also a few links embedded in the text. You can click them for additional information, but that's optional.

Omer: Remember that I will be seeing these results, so please take it seriously and do your best work. I think you'll enjoy it.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> interdisciplinary_orientation

Introduction to Comm360 [id=introduction]
------------------------

You: So I'm starting my internship at Community 360 today. I'm a bit nervous but excited.

Teammate: Me too! I heard it's called Comm360 for short. They do really interesting work in the community.

You: Yeah, their mission is to address community-based needs through responsive programming. They work in education, health and wellness, family and youth services, and community building.

Teammate: I read that a community needs survey identified high school dropout rates as a serious issue here. That's what we'll be working on.

You: Right. We've been asked to investigate the causes of high school dropout so Comm360 can develop services to address the problem. This sounds like it'll be challenging work.

Teammate: Before we dive in, we should probably figure out who's on our team. There should be a team directory around here somewhere. Want to explore it and see who we'll be working with?

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> interdisciplinary_team_intro

Meet the Team [id=team_intro]
-------------

Teammate: Great! So from the directory, it looks like our intern teammates are Ty, Peggy, and Lacy.

You: And we'll all be working together to identify key concepts, review readings, and organize information for a report about what causes young people to drop out of high school.

Teammate: Our supervisor is Lianne Park. I saw she's been at Comm360 for five years and directs multiple programs. She'll present our findings to the Program Development and Evaluation committee.

You: And she reports to Anne Hastings, the CEO, who makes the ultimate decisions about programs and resources.

Teammate: Now that we know the team, let's see what Lianne has for us to work on.

Task Overview [id=task_list]
-------------

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> interdisciplinary_task_list

Lianne: Welcome to the team, everyone. We're glad you're here at Comm360 to help us work on this problem. Take a look at the handout. Over the next 45 to 60 minutes, you'll work together to complete these tasks.


Lianne: By the way, you can call me Lianne. We're pretty informal here.

Lacy: Thanks, Lianne.

Task 1: Brainstorm [id=brainstorm_start]
-----------------------

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> cause_brainstorming

Lianne: Let's get started with the brainstorming task.

Lianne: When someone drops out of high school and never gets a diploma or a GED, it has negative consequences, as you know.

Lianne: Why don't each of you start by reviewing what you know about the causes of people dropping out of high school?

Lianne: It may be from personal experiences of people you know or know of.

Lianne: When you're done, we'll put all the ideas together as a way to start looking into research about the causes of dropping out.

Lacy: Thanks, Lianne. I know some old classmates who left high school before graduating, but I didn't exactly think it was my place to ask them why they dropped out.

Ty: Me too. Maybe we should each take a few minutes to think of different reasons or causes of dropping out of high school.

Ty: When we're done, we can combine them and then evaluate which might be most valuable for conducting research into the issue, like Lianne suggested.

Ty: I'm just not sure how to go about it.

Peggy: I have an idea for that. Let's write single sentences for each cause. I'll start. I had a friend who was in a car accident freshman year of high school. She had broken bones, trauma, and couldn't even leave her house for almost two months.

Peggy: She fell so far behind that by the time she got back, she just couldn't figure out how to catch up.

Ty: How could we put that into one sentence?

Lacy: How about... Students who have illnesses that make it hard for them to keep up with schoolwork.

Peggy: That works for me. Let's see if each of us can come up with at least two different causes for dropping out of high school.

Peggy: Lianne, what do you think?

Lianne: That's a good plan, but keep in mind, not all causes start with students or their families.

Lacy: What do you mean?

Lianne: Well, for example, there are cases where schools, like in rural communities, can't accommodate students with certain physical or mental disabilities. In those cases, families simply have no reasonable option for allowing their child to continue to graduation.

Lianne: This is an example of a structural cause, not one situated in the individual.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> internal_and_structural_causes

Lianne: Keep an open mind about possible causes because our programs can work with multiple stakeholders, like schools and community groups as well as families and students.

Ty: Ah, I get it. So, why don't we each write one reason that is more like a structural cause and one that is focused more on the individual?

Peggy: That's a good idea. I'll try it even though I'm not quite sure I get the difference yet.

Peggy: So let's see if we can each write two causes.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> cause_inputs

--- wait @cause_internal.value && @cause_structural.value ---

[id=causes_review]
You: I'm done.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> causes_list

Lacy: Here are all of our reasons, Lianne. What do you think?

Lianne: This is a good list. It reflects a lot of what we heard in a focus group we conducted after we got the results of our community needs survey. We spoke to various stakeholders - students and their families, teachers, and other community members. 

Task 2: Review Focus Group Data [id=focus_group_start]
---------------------------------------

Lianne: Now let's move on to the next task. I'd like you to take a look at part of the transcript from that focus group to extract additional reasons because this is an extremely complex problem, as you've already identified, with many causes.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> transcript_md1

Lianne: Read the transcript before you go on.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> focus_group_transcript_pt1

Lianne: Highlight any reasons that our participants gave for students dropping out of high school, and add a note explaining why it describes a cause of high school dropout. You'll be able to use these notes later.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> focus_group_highlights_shown

Ty: I hope we get to work on focus groups here because that was really interesting. Here's what I highlighted.

Task 3: Select Focus Areas [id=focus_selection]
---------------------

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> reasons_list

Lacy: I studied focus groups in my research methods class last year. We analyzed lots of transcripts. Ty, I'm taking your highlights and combining them with the reasons we already came up with. I re-worded some of them, but I think I've captured everything.

Lianne: Good work. This list reflects what we've heard in the community about this issue. Now we need to turn our attention to the research. To do that, we need to have an idea what disciplines might be relevant to our problem. Why don't you all look over the list again, and come up with a list of disciplines that might have something to say about one or more of the causes we listed?

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> brainstorming_disciplines


Lianne: Great work! However, I think we should focus on a subset of these reasons -- ones that Comm360 might actually be able to address.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> reasons_checklist

Lacy: Which ones would you select to research? Pick at least three to start with, and no more than five.

--- wait @reasons.value.length > 2 && @reasons.value.length < 6 ---

Lianne: I agree with you on some of your choices. Why did you select that set? Why do you think Comm360 would be able to address them?

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> reasons_shown

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> reason_explanation_input

--- wait @reason_explanation.value ---

Lianne: I appreciate the thoughtfulness you've shown on this issue, both as individuals and as a team.

Lianne: I reviewed the reasons you put together with Ms. Hastings. We agree that this subset of causes are appropriate for us to consider as an organization to build programmatic outreach and solutions.

Lianne: The causes we'll focus on are: students needing to earn money right away to help their family, conditions like depression, anxiety, or ADHD interfering with schoolwork, environmental factors in areas affected by gang activity and limited support systems, and lack of school funding affecting resources for underserved students.

Task 4: Reflect -- Is This Interdisciplinary? [id=interdisciplinary_check]
--------------------------------------

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> task_set_3_intro

Ty: This list cuts across a lot of different subjects. I'm looking forward to seeing what kind of research we'll find for each of them.

Lianne: Yes, and we'll move into the research phase of this work shortly, but first I'd like us to discuss our approach. At Comm360, we typically use an interdisciplinary approach, the one developed by Repko and colleagues, in 2019. In the past, this approach has allowed us to combine insights to come to a holistic solution for the issue we're tackling.

Lianne: So we'll need to determine if the problem of people dropping out of high school is the kind of problem that is appropriate for interdisciplinary research.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> broad_model_display

Peggy: Ah, I think all of us are familiar with Broad Model from Repko from Professor Ari.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> first_pass_on_criteria

Lianne: Of course. Which means you should be able to see how the high school dropout model fits the model! Why don't you all take a moment and see how well it fits Repko's criteria.

Peggy: I'm looking at our answers, and it looks like we've got some common themes. For starters, there are a lot of causes of high school dropout, and it's obviously important unresolved!

Ty: Yes, and the fact that there are so many causes means it's a complex problem. There won't be any one simple solution for it.

Lacy: Plus there are a lot of different kinds of causes, which means there's a good chance you'll need information from several different disciplines to solve it.

Peggy: OK then. Based on the Broad Model, it looks like next, we need to look at the causes on the list and see if they map to different disciplines.

Ty: And we should identify the disciplines we think would give us the most insights into the problem and possible solutions.

Ty: Let's do it.

Peggy: Let's figure out which disciplines are likely to give us the most insights into the problem and possible solutions. How about each of us select all the disciplines in a row that we think apply for each of the causes listed? I think it is ok if we check as many boxes in a row as we think apply. Then, we can sum up our responses to see if they align and if we think this is an interdisciplinary problem.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> causes_by_disciplines

// HACK: Object.keys().length is instructor-unfriendly. TODO: Add isFilled() or .numberFilled field
--- wait Object.keys(@discipline_mapping.value).length > 3 ---

Peggy: Ok, I summed up our responses and this shows any category that got more than one vote. We didn't always agree on each discipline, but overall we all seem to see how different disciplines might investigate the problem.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> causes_by_disciplines_answers

Lacy: I agree. Our analysis supports what you were saying, Ty. These causes cut across a lot of subjects.

Ty: It seems like an interdisciplinary approach makes sense in this case.

Peggy: I also agree. It's a really complex problem, so the Repko approach should help Comm360 study it from a lot of different angles.

Lianne: We all agree that taking an interdisciplinary approach will work to study a complex problem like young people dropping out of school.

Lianne: Let's dig in a bit now and get more information about some of the causes. We should be able to locate studies from some of the disciplines we have identified that address different parts of the problem.

Ty: I'm already on it. I took a few key words from one of the causes and did a search. One study looks promising.

Task 5: Review Literature - ADHD Study [id=literature_adhd]
----------------------------------------------

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> adhd_study_text

Ty: Here it is.

Lacy: This does look promising. Which discipline, or maybe disciplines, are represented in this study? I mean, which ones are the most relevant, if we have these to choose from? Health sciences, biology, psychology, sociology, economics, or political science?

Peggy: I think it's Psychology. There are some clues and jargon in the summary.

Ty: Can you show us?

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> adhd_study_highlighted

Peggy: So I highlighted the clues and jargon that makes me think this study is in the Psychology field. Attention, learning disorders, and IQ are all concepts that psychologists typically study.

Peggy: Plus there's a heavy focus on early identification and intervention, especially by educational psychologists.

Peggy: A big clue was also psychological risk factors.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> adhd_study_text

Lianne: Great! Thanks for that, Peggy. Now let's look at the article again.

Lianne: As you recall, when interdisciplinarians locate a disciplinary source, they identify
the "insight" of that source. This "insight" is often a recommendation, main finding, or
conclusion offered by the authors of the disciplinary source and reflects the perspective
of the discipline. What do you think is the main insight of this source?

Lacy: It is clearly about ADHD and that students with ADHD are more likely to drop out of high school.

Peggy: That is true, but the source also lists social class and IQ as predictors of dropping out.
Shouldn't we add that to the sentence you started?

Ty: Yeah, but ADHD status continues to predict high school dropout even after controlling for
social class and IQ. So I am not sure about adding them. Instead, we should add something about
early intervention; the source repeatedly mentions the importance of early intervention.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> adhd_insight_sentence_tabs

Lianne: Great discussion, everyone! Let's try to capture the insight more clearly than
we have so far. Why don't we all write a sentence?

--- wait @adhd_insight_sentence.value ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> adhd_insight_sentence_choice

Lianne: Good work. Here are the sentences we suggested. Which one should we go with?

--- wait @adhd_insight_sentence_choice_input.value ---

Lianne: I agree with you all that ADHD status is the main predictor of high school dropout according to this study. I also think early intervention is strongly recommended.

Lianne: Therefore we should go with this sentence for insight of this source:

Lianne: **ADHD is a key risk factor for dropout, and early intervention can reduce this risk.**

Task 5: Literature Review -- Employment Study [id=literature_employment]
----------------

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> employment_study_text

Lacy: I used the search term method and found another study. This one is on employment. Take a look.

Lianne: See if you can find the clue words that indicate which disciplines are relevant in this study.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> employment_study_annotation_screen

Lianne: So which disciplines do you think are represented in this study? Highlight clue words or phrases in the text. Then click on "Annotate", go to the right, and add a note saying what discipline the clue suggests, and why.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> employment_discipline_checkboxes

Lianne: Based on your answers, check the disciplines represented in this study.

--- wait @source_2_classification.value ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> source_2_explanation_input

Lianne: How are the clues you picked representative of the disciplines you picked? Can you explain?

--- wait @source_2_classification_explanation.value ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> employment_study_text

Lianne: As we did with the ADHD study, we need to identify the "insight of this source".

Lianne: Recall that disciplinary insight is often a recommendation, main finding,
or conclusion offered by the authors of the disciplinary source and it reflects
the perspective of the discipline.

Ty: It says there are significant risks to students who work more than 20 hours.

Lacy: Yeah, but only 11% of students worked more than 20 hours in the study during the school year.

Peggy: It also cites overrepresentation of disadvantaged youth in the high risk group.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> source_2_insight_input

Lianne: Why don't we each write our own sentence like we did for the other study?

--- wait @source_2_insight_sentence.value ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> employment_insight_sentence_choice

Lianne: Great work. Here are the sentences you all suggested. Which one should we go with?

--- wait @employment_insight_sentence_choice_input.value ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> source_2_insight_explanation_screen

Lianne: Could you explain why you chose the sentence you picked?

--- wait @source_2_insight_explanation.value ---

Lianne: Great discussion. Unlike the other sentences, the following sentence not only
establishes intensive work as a risk factor, but also calls on schools to support
students who have to work long hours. Thus it captures the entirety of the insight
offered by the study --

Lianne: **Intensive work during high school remains a risk factor for dropout, and
schools should provide support and flexibility for students who must work long hours.**

Task 5: Literature Review -- Rural and Urban Study [id=literature_rural_urban]
---------------------

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> rural_urban_study_text

Lacy: I think it makes sense to use this highlighting method for other studies we find. Like this one.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> rural_discipline_checkboxes

Lianne: So which disciplines do you think are represented in this study?

--- wait @source_3_classification.value ---

Lianne: How are the clues you picked representative of the study you picked. Can you explain?

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> source_3_explanation_input

--- wait @source_3_classification_explanation.value ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> reading_rural_urban

Lianne: What do you think is the disciplinary insight of this source?

Lianne: Remember that disciplinary "insight" is often a recommendation, main finding,
or conclusion offered by the authors of the disciplinary source and it reflects the
perspective of the discipline.

Ty: It says that dropout rates have gotten lower but that they are largely similar
between rural and urban areas.

Lacy: That means that geography does not matter that much, at least according to this study.

Peggy: Correct. The summary notes that other factors are more important such as family characteristics
and peer influences.

Ty: Exactly! For key determinants, it lists things like gender, family assets, presence of
biological parents, and maternal education.

Lacy: And for peer influences it lists gang involvement and drug use.

Peggy: These should be included in the study insight, which should also include
policy implications of addressing family and peer influences to improve graduation rates.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> source_3_insight

Lianne: These are all great points. How about we each write our own sentence and pick
the one that captures these points the best?

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> rural_insight_checkboxes

--- wait @source_3_insight_selection.value ---

Lianne: Great work. Here are the sentences you suggested. Which one should we go with?

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> source_3_insight_explanation

Lianne: You chose the following sentence. Why did you select that one?

--- wait @source_3_insight_sentence_explanation.value ---

Lianne: Thank you, everyone. The following sentence includes the main points about family
and peer factors and policy reforms that are needed to address them.

Lianne: **High school dropout rates are similar in rural and urban areas, with family and peer factors
being more important than geography in predicting graduation, underscoring the need for policies that
address these influences.**

Task 5: Literature Review -- School Funding Study [id=literature_funding]
--------------------

Ty: I'm going to add this study to the ones we should review.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> funding_study_text

Lianne: So which disciplines do you think are represented in this study?

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> funding_study_additional_disciplines_checkboxes

--- wait @funding_study_additional_disciplines.value ---

Lianne: How are the clues you picked representative of the disciplines you selected? Can you explain?

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> source_4_explanation_input

--- wait @source_4_classification_explanation.value ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> funding_study_text_md

Lianne: How about the disciplinary insight of this source?

Ty: This study is about an underfunded school district in Delaware.

Lacy: And it is focused on African-American students, highlighting low-income and minority status
as risk factors.

Peggy: True. These factors are important but the main focus is on school funding.
As the summary states, "students in underfunded schools face increased risks due to
fewer academic and support services."

Lianne: Yes, and the author argues for school finance reform included weighted student
funding models to more equitably allocate funding to high-need schools.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> source_4_insight_input

Lianne: Let's write a sentence for the insight of this fourth source.

--- wait @source_4_classification_explanation.value ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> source_4_insight_selection_input

Lianne: Good work. Here are the sentences you all suggested. Which one should we go with?

--- wait @source4_insight_selection.value ---

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar -> source_4_insight_explanation_input

Lianne: Why did you choose this sentence? Explain.

--- wait @source_4_insight_sentence_explanation.value ---

Lianne: Thank you all. We should go with the following sentence --

Lianne: **Inadequate school funding is linked to higher dropout rates among African American students, underscoring the need for funding reforms to improve resources in high-poverty schools.**

Lianne: It conveys both the central finding of the study and its broader implication. It identifies the key result—that inadequate school funding is linked to higher dropout rates among African American students—while also emphasizing why this matters by pointing to the need for funding reforms in high-poverty schools.

Lianne: Good work! This ends Part 1 of the SBA. Let's recap what we have done so far.

Lianne: We brainstormed and learned causes of high school dropout. That helped us understand
that this is a complex problem with multiple parts, which makes it suitable for interdisciplinary
study. We then located sources from different disciplines shedding light and insight on those causes.

